<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Android 11-and-lower form of the rules in `data_extraction_rules.xml`,
  which is where the reasoning lives.

  No device this app runs on reads this file. `android:fullBackupContent` is
  consulted up to Android 11 (API 30) and `android:dataExtractionRules` from
  Android 12 (API 31) onwards, while `minSdk` is 33, so every supported device
  takes the other file and ignores this one. It is kept in step rather than left
  to drift, because a stale copy of a security-relevant rule is worse company
  than none: the next reader has no way to tell which of the two is live.

  Deleting it, and the `android:fullBackupContent` attribute that points here,
  looks like the honest end state and is not. Both are deliberately kept.

  What they are is a safety net for a change nobody would connect to backups:
  lowering `minSdk` to 30 or below, which is an ordinary thing to do to reach
  more devices. On those API levels `android:dataExtractionRules` is ignored, and
  an app with no `android:fullBackupContent` gets *unrestricted* full backup:
  the passphrase-less SSH key and the server's connection token included, off the
  device and into the cloud. Nothing would fail, nothing would be logged, and the
  cause would be a manifest edit from months earlier.

  So this file is not dead weight so much as a floor. Keep it identical to the
  `<cloud-backup>` section of `data_extraction_rules.xml`; the two disagreeing is
  the only way this arrangement can quietly go wrong.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="file" path="home/.vscodroid/data/Machine" />
</full-backup-content>
